-- title:
--   Lemma 7.3 — perfect completeness of the folded reduction
-- statement:
--   Given a finite ordering instance with a basic LP local model, a two-to-one game, and consistent supported sampling matrices of the game's alphabet length, if the game has value one then all constraints of the folded ordering test of §7.3 can be satisfied simultaneously. Explicitly, the quotient of long-code entries by the equivalence relation generated by edge pullbacks has an injective natural rank satisfying every base-constraint test at every left vertex and every column permutation. Quotient classes from different base variables are distinct. The assertion concerns this particular folded test, not an unspecified reduction.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: finite indexed sets [n] and positive normalized weighted ordering instances → zero-based Fin indices, indexed repeated constraints, injective scopes, and strictly positive real weights of total one; reason: reindexing changes no instances; isolated variables remain allowed and positive total mass excludes an empty constraint list
--   - encoding: an ordering of the folded output variables → an injective natural-valued rank on the explicitly generated quotient satisfying every positive-weight test; reason: the quotient is finite, so such ranks and permutations give the same total orders
--   - generalization: the regularized sparse SDP base and rich source game fixed in §7 → any basic LP local model with supported sampling matrices and any two-to-one game; reason: the completeness proof uses supported satisfying columns and edge identifications only; the source data specialize to these hypotheses
-- source:
--   Yury Makarychev (2026), Optimal Approximation Algorithms For Satisfiable Ordering CSPs, held STOC 2027 submission, sha256:e49b7905adb41a36b93abcbd7818d1b18ca86a77dff069da884ba2f8e85775cc, Lemma 7.3, printed p. 43 (PDF p. 46); citation_basis: published.

